  "query_preview": "..\n\nDry and stable conditions exist locally late this afternoon, \nsustained as a 1035 mb surface high holds firm within the background \nof lower amplitude upper ridging. Seasonably cool resident thermal \nprofile will remain entrenched through tonight, marked by a sub-zero \n850 mb temperatures. Window of clear sky through at least the first \nhalf of the night will offer a period of favorable radiational \ncooling with a veering/light northeast gradient. Lows again \nWednesday morning arriving generally in the low to mid 20s. Flow off \nthe warmer lake Huron waters will support some degree of lake \nstratus development during the early morning hours. Dry and \nseasonable conditions will exist Wednesday and Wed night as high \npressure maintains some influence while gradually sliding to the \neast. Limited moderation to the thermal profile expected this period \nas low level flow maintains an easterly component and cloud cover \nperpetuates at times. High Wednesday in the low to mid 40s.  \n\nCompact mid level noted on water vapor ejecting across southern \nAlberta remains forecast to dig into the central conus over the next \n48 hours. This will occur as a secondary wave ejects into the \nsouthern plains and gradually deepens. Some model variability \nremains in handling the pace and degree of interaction between these \nfeatures. In general, expectation for a deep closed system to emerge \nover the Tennessee Valley by early Thursday, with the main height \nfall center then lifting northeast thru the Ohio valley through \nFriday. This favorably positions southeast Michigan for a widespread \nprecipitation event centered mainly from 18z Thu to 18z Fri. This \nwill occur as deeper mid level moisture transport develops within an \narea of strong deformation along the north flank of the upper low. \nLatest sounding data and thickness levels still point to primary a \nliquid ptype, but with a window late Thursday night/Fri morning fir \na possible mix to emerge as freezing levels lower and become more \nsupportive. Any accumulation would be very minor and unimpactful as \ntemperatures remain solidly above freezing. Rainfall totals \ntargeting the .5 to 1\" range, while acknowledging the 75th \npercentile of ensemble guidance does approach 1.25\" across the \nsouth. Daylight temperatures for Thanksgiving and Friday again \nparked in the 40s. Winds gusting into the 25 to 30 mph range each \nday.  \n\nEnergetic upper level flow of north pacific origin lends to \nadditional shortwave energy funneling downstream during the latter \nhalf of the holiday weekend. An ill-defined environment overall, but \ncertainly presents some opportunity for additional rain production \nat times. No real signal for any meaningful adjustment to the \nthermal profile as flow generally hold as zonal. This will ensure \ntemperatures finish the weekend hovering near average for the end of \nNovember.",
